# [Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree/)

## Approach
- If the current node is `NULL`, return `nullptr`.
- If the current node matches either `p` or `q`, return the current node.
- Recursively search for `p` and `q` in the left and right subtrees.
- If both subtrees return non-null nodes, the current node is the Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA).
- Otherwise, return whichever subtree result is non-null.

## Code
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode* root, TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {
        if (!root) return nullptr;
        if (root == p) return p;
        if (root == q) return q;

        TreeNode* left_node = lowestCommonAncestor(root->left, p, q);
        TreeNode* right_node = lowestCommonAncestor(root->right, p, q);

        if (left_node && right_node) return root;

        return left_node ? left_node : right_node;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), where \(n\) is the number of nodes in the binary tree, since each node is visited at most once.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(h)\), where \(h\) is the height of the tree, due to the recursive call stack. In the worst case, \(O(n)\) for a skewed tree.
